Port nn.ConvTranspose2d (stride 1, padding 0, output_padding 0, dilation 1, groups 1, no bias) as a plain JAX reference. I left the KernelBench default sizes as they are because they already meet the size rules. The input x is (64, 64, 128, 256) f32, which is 536.9 MB, inside the 50–600 MB range. Batch is 64, and the channel and spatial sizes are multiples of 64 or 128. The output is (64, 128, 130, 260), about 1.1 GB.

The weight keeps PyTorch's ConvTranspose layout (Cin, Cout, kH, kW) = (64, 128, 3, 5) at the interface, initialised as normal * 0.02. Inside `workload`, the transposed conv is written as an ordinary convolution:
- flip the kernel spatially and reorder it to HWIO (kH, kW, Cin, Cout);
- set lhs_dilation to the stride and rhs_dilation to the dilation;
- pad each spatial side by dilation*(k-1) - padding, with output_padding added on the high side;
- convert NCHW to NHWC on the way in and back again on the way out.

This gives an output size of (H-1)*s - 2p + d*(k-1) + op + 1, which matches PyTorch. Stride, padding, output_padding and dilation are all stored in CONFIG. The port only handles groups=1.

